      "dom_id": "question-66431",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The equation ${x}^{2}-10x+50=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+50 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x & =-50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x & =-50 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =-50 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-25 & =-50 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5,\\,q=-25,$ and $p+q=-30.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-25} \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 5\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =5\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=5+5\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=5-5\\,\\text{i}.$",
